# Three build-time values, for running the cockpit on your own machine.
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in whichever of them you want; `.env`
# itself is never tracked, and `.gitignore` keeps it that way.
#
# This file is for local development and nothing else. In production the
# same three values come from the repository's own variables, which
# `deploy.yml`, `preview.yml` and `publish-showcase.yml` read at build
# time, and `declarations/standing-up.yml` declares the step that sets
# them.
#
# Every value below is blank, and blank is a working state rather than an
# unfinished one -- that is D-13, and each comment says what its own value
# does when it is left empty. The application runs with all three empty.
#
# Warning: nothing here is a secret, and nothing here may become one. The
# `VITE_` prefix is what makes that a rule rather than an observation --
# Vite substitutes the value into the bundle at build time, so whatever is
# written here is downloaded, in the clear, by every visitor to the
# published site. That is harmless for these three, which are two addresses
# and a public client id a browser sends anyway. It is not harmless for the
# things a file called `.env` invites somebody to put beside them: a token,
# a password, a signing key. No variable in this application holds one, and
# adding one here would publish it.

# Leave both blank to sign in with a personal access token.
# Filling both switches the app to the device flow -- no code change needed.
VITE_AUTH_PROXY_URL=
VITE_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=

# The signup relay's URL (services/signup-relay/). Leave blank and the
# registration form still encrypts, but reports registration as not open
# yet rather than sending anywhere -- a normal state (D-13), not an error.
VITE_SIGNUP_RELAY_URL=
